Autonomous Capability Transition Planning for Multiplanetary Infrastructure

A Closed-Loop Decision Framework for Sequencing Interdependent Industrial Capabilities Under Resource, Uncertainty, and Resilience Constraints

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

Establishing sustained human presence beyond Earth requires the progressive development of interconnected industrial capabilities rather than isolated autonomous systems. While substantial research has addressed individual components—autonomous construction, ISRU processes, multi-robot coordination, and logistics optimization—a critical gap remains at the systems level: how should an autonomous decision architecture determine which industrial capability to develop next, given the current state of a deployed infrastructure system and its desired future trajectory?

This dissertation presents a theoretically grounded framework for autonomous capability transition planning in multiplanetary infrastructure contexts. The core contribution is the formalization of capability sequencing as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) over a dynamically expanding dependency graph, where the action space itself evolves as new capabilities are deployed. This represents a fundamental shift from static technology roadmapping and fixed bootstrapping templates toward closed-loop, state-dependent decision-making under physical, resource, and reliability constraints.

The framework integrates four previously disparate bodies of work: capability-based planning from defense systems engineering, Theory of Constraints from operations research, constrained reinforcement learning from machine learning, and space logistics network optimization. The novelty lies not in any single component but in their synthesis into a domain-specific decision architecture that can adapt to stochastic failures, resource limitations, and the structural self-modification of its own action space.

The autonomy objective is rigorously defined using maximum network flow, providing formal monotonicity guarantees under graph inclusion. A novel Constrained Progressive Widening Monte Carlo Tree Search (CPW-MCTS) algorithm handles the non-stationary action space with finite-sample regret bounds. A hybrid bottleneck identification scheme integrates continuous sensitivity analysis with discrete graph-expansion scoring to guide exploration. The framework is validated through extensive simulation against five named baselines, including a globally optimized static sequence, under stochastic fault injection scenarios.

This dissertation contributes to autonomous systems engineering by providing a mathematically principled, computationally feasible decision framework for capability sequencing in self-expanding infrastructure systems. The results demonstrate that closed-loop replanning significantly outperforms open-loop static planning under uncertainty, and that the proposed algorithm achieves high autonomy per unit Earth-delivered mass while satisfying safety constraints.

