Chapter 5: Mathematical Foundations

5.1 Constrained Markov Decision Process Formalization

The capability transition planner is formulated as a CMDP defined by the tuple:

\mathcal{M} = \langle \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{P}, R, \{C_k\}_{k=1}^K, \mathbf{c}, \gamma, \rho_0 \rangle

5.1.1 State Space  \mathcal{S} 

The state space is a hybrid continuous-discrete space:

\mathcal{S} = \mathbb{R}^7_+ \times [0,1]^7 \times \mathbb{R}^m_+ \times \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathcal{G}

where  \mathcal{G}  is the space of all possible directed acyclic graphs over  V_H .

A state  s_t \in \mathcal{S}  is the tuple:

s_t = \langle \mathbf{C}_t, \mathbf{K}_t, \mathbf{D}_t, \mathbf{E}_t, G_t \rangle

Continuous Operational Capacities  \mathbf{C}_t \in \mathbb{R}^7_+ :

\mathbf{C}_t = [C_{\text{power}}, C_{\text{excav}}, C_{\text{refine}}, C_{\text{manuf}}, C_{\text{trans}}, C_{\text{constr}}, C_{\text{maint}}]

with units specified in Table 5.1.

Component Symbol Unit Description
Power  C_{\text{power}}  kW Power generation capacity
Excavation  C_{\text{excav}}  t/day Regolith excavation rate
Refining  C_{\text{refine}}  t/day Material refining throughput
Manufacturing  C_{\text{manuf}}  kg/day Manufacturing output
Transport  C_{\text{trans}}  t·km/day Transport capacity
Construction  C_{\text{constr}}  m³/day Construction rate
Maintenance  C_{\text{maint}}  repairs/day Maintenance throughput

Health Indices  \mathbf{K}_t \in [0,1]^7 : each component  K_{i,t}  represents the operational health of subsystem  i , with 1 fully operational, 0 complete failure, and intermediate values degraded performance.

Resource Inventory  \mathbf{D}_t \in \mathbb{R}^m_+ :

\mathbf{D}_t = [D_{\text{raw\_regolith}}, D_{\text{extracted\_metals}}, D_{\text{sintered\_blocks}}, D_{\text{spare\_parts}}]

Cumulative Earth Mass  \mathbf{E}_t \in \mathbb{R}_+ : total mass (kg) imported from Earth up to time  t .

Dependency Graph  G_t = (V_t, E_t) : the active capability graph where  V_t \subseteq V_H  are deployed capabilities and  E_t \subseteq E_H  active dependencies.

5.1.2 Action Space  \mathcal{A}(s_t) 

The action space is state-dependent due to graph dynamics:

\mathcal{A}(s_t) = \{ a_t = \langle v_{\text{target}}, \mathbf{m}_{\text{import}}, \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}} \rangle \mid v_{\text{target}} \in V_{\text{candidate}}(G_t) \}

Candidate Capabilities:

V_{\text{candidate}}(G_t) = \{ v \in V_H \setminus V_t \mid \forall u \in \text{parents}(v): u \in V_t \}

This ensures all prerequisite capabilities are present before a new capability is considered.

Import Payload Vector  \mathbf{m}_{\text{import}} \in \mathbb{R}^m_+ : mass required from Earth for the transition (structural, avionics, tooling, spares).

Capacity Allocation Ratios  \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}} \in [0,1]^7 : fraction of each current capacity dedicated to building  v_{\text{target}} . The complement  1 - \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}}  is dedicated to ongoing maintenance and operations.

5.1.3 Transition Dynamics  \mathcal{P} 

State transitions follow a dual-rate stochastic trajectory integrating micro-step dynamics over each macro-step interval. Define the micro-step time index  \tau \in [t, t+\Delta T]  with resolution  \Delta t . The full transition kernel is:

\mathcal{P}(s_{t+1} \mid s_t, a_t) = \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\omega} \sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{lunar}}} \left[ \delta \left( s_{t+1} - \left( s_t + \int_{\tau=t}^{t+\Delta T} \psi(s_\tau, a_t, \boldsymbol{\omega}_\tau) \, d\tau \right) \right) \right]

where  \psi(\cdot)  is the differential system drift comprising:

Continuous Capacity Dynamics:

\frac{d\mathbf{C}_\tau}{d\tau} = \underbrace{\boldsymbol{\eta}(v_{\text{target}}) \cdot \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}} \cdot \mathbf{C}_\tau}_{\text{construction gain}} - \underbrace{\boldsymbol{\lambda} \odot (1 - \mathbf{K}_\tau) \odot \mathbf{C}_\tau}_{\text{degradation}}

where  \boldsymbol{\eta}(v_{\text{target}}) \in \mathbb{R}^7_+  is the efficiency vector for building capability  v_{\text{target}} , and  \boldsymbol{\lambda} \in \mathbb{R}^7_+  the natural degradation rate vector.

Health Dynamics:

\frac{dK_{i,\tau}}{d\tau} = -\alpha_i - \beta_i \, r_{\text{alloc},i} + \gamma_i \, r_{\text{maint}} + \omega_{i,\tau}

with  \alpha_i  natural degradation,  \beta_i  accelerated wear,  \gamma_i  recovery rate, and  \omega_{i,\tau}  stochastic environmental disturbance.

Resource Inventory Dynamics:

\frac{d\mathbf{D}_\tau}{d\tau} = \underbrace{\mathbf{P}(G_\tau) \cdot \mathbf{C}_\tau}_{\text{production}} - \underbrace{\mathbf{Q}(v_{\text{target}}) \cdot \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}} \cdot \mathbf{C}_\tau}_{\text{consumption for construction}} - \underbrace{\mathbf{Q}_{\text{ops}}(G_\tau)}_{\text{operational consumption}}

Graph Update (Discrete Event): the graph is augmented when cumulative resource allocation exceeds the build requirement:

G_{t+1} = \begin{cases}
(V_t \cup \{v_{\text{target}}\}, E_t \cup \text{edges}(v_{\text{target}})) & \text{if } \int_{\tau=t}^{t+\Delta T} \mathbf{Q}(v_{\text{target}}) \cdot \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}} \cdot \mathbf{C}_\tau \, d\tau \geq \mathbf{Q}_{\text{build}}(v_{\text{target}}) \\
G_t & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

For computational tractability, the integral is approximated via forward Euler with  \Delta t = 1  hour, giving  N_{\text{micro}} = \Delta T / \Delta t = 720  micro-steps per macro-step.

5.1.4 Reward Function  R 

We define the reward as:

R(s_t, a_t, s_{t+1}) = \Phi(G_{t+1}) - \Phi(G_t) - \alpha \left( \frac{\|\mathbf{m}_{\text{import}}\|_1}{\Phi_{\text{target}}} \right) - \beta \cdot \text{Risk}(a_t)

where  \Phi(G)  is the autonomy function,  \Phi_{\text{target}}  a normalization constant,  \alpha, \beta > 0  weighting coefficients, and Risk defined below.

Autonomy Function  \Phi(G_t)  — Maximum Network Flow Formulation

The autonomy function is defined as the normalized maximum flow through the active capability graph. To model node capacities correctly, each node  v \in V_t  is split into two vertices  v_{\text{in}}  and  v_{\text{out}}  connected by an edge  (v_{\text{in}}, v_{\text{out}})  with capacity equal to the node's operational capacity  C_{v,t} . All incoming edges connect to  v_{\text{in}} , all outgoing from  v_{\text{out}} . A virtual source  s  connects to all source nodes (capabilities that produce resources without input) with capacity  C_{u,t} ; a virtual sink  t^*  receives from all sink nodes (capabilities that are final system outputs, defined as nodes with no outgoing edges in the meta-graph or designated as system outputs). Internal edge capacities between  u_{\text{out}}  and  v_{\text{in}}  are set to  \min(C_{u,t}, C_{v,t}) .

The maximum flow value is:

\text{MaxFlow}(\mathbf{C}_t, G_t) = \max_{\mathbf{f}} \sum_{v \in V_{\text{snk}}} f(v_{\text{out}}, t^*)

subject to capacity and conservation constraints.

Then:

\Phi(G_t) \triangleq \frac{\text{MaxFlow}(\mathbf{C}_t, G_t)}{\Phi_{\text{target}}}

where  \Phi_{\text{target}}  is the maximum achievable flow with full meta-graph deployment and nominal capacities.

Risk Function:

\text{Risk}(a_t) = 1 - \prod_{i \in \text{prereq}(v_{\text{target}})} K_{i,t}^{\alpha_i}

with  \alpha_i > 0  sensitivity weights.

5.1.5 Constraint Functions  \{C_k\}_{k=1}^3 

1. Cumulative Payload Mass Limit:  C_1 = \|\mathbf{m}_{\text{import}}\|_1 , with  \mathbb{E}_\pi [\sum \gamma^t C_1] \le M_{\max} .
2. Energy Reserve Floor:  C_2 = \max(0, E_{\text{required}} - E_{\text{available}}) , with expected discounted sum  \le 0 .
3. Systemic Risk Threshold:  C_3 = 1 - \min(\mathbf{K}_{t+1}) , with  \mathbb{E}_\pi [\sum \gamma^t C_3] \le 1 - K_{\min} .

5.1.6 Constrained Policy Optimization

The objective is:

\pi^* = \arg\max_\pi \mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^T \gamma^t R(s_t, a_t) \right]

subject to  \mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^T \gamma^t C_k(s_t, a_t) \right] \le c_k, \; \forall k \in \{1,2,3\} .

Lagrangian Formulation: the constrained problem is converted to an unconstrained one via Lagrange multipliers  \lambda_k \ge 0 :

\mathcal{L}(\pi, \boldsymbol{\lambda}) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^T \gamma^t \left( R(s_t, a_t) - \sum_{k=1}^3 \lambda_k C_k(s_t, a_t) \right) \right]

The dual problem is  \min_{\boldsymbol{\lambda} \ge 0} \max_\pi \mathcal{L}(\pi, \boldsymbol{\lambda}) . For CPW-MCTS, the penalty is applied directly in the action selection criterion.

