Chapter 6: Theoretical Guarantees

6.1 Policy Existence

Theorem 1: Given a finite meta-graph  V_H  with  |V_H| = N < \infty , there exists at least one feasible policy.

Proof: The trivial policy  \pi_0  that selects NO_OP at every step satisfies all constraints trivially. ∎

6.2 Monotonicity of Autonomy Function Under Maximum Flow

Theorem 2 (Monotonicity under Graph Inclusion): Let  H = (V_H, E_H)  be the total meta-graph. Let  G_a = (V_a, E_a)  and  G_b = (V_b, E_b)  be two active subgraphs with capacity vectors  \mathbf{C}_a  and  \mathbf{C}_b . If  G_a \subseteq G_b  and  \mathbf{C}_a \le \mathbf{C}_b  pointwise, then  \Phi(G_a) \le \Phi(G_b) .

Proof: The proof uses the max-flow formulation with node splitting. An optimal flow  \mathbf{f}^*_a  in  \mathcal{N}_a  can be extended to a feasible flow  \mathbf{f}'_b  in  \mathcal{N}_b  by setting zero flow on new edges. Since capacities in  \mathcal{N}_b  are at least as large on all shared edges and non-negative on new edges,  \mathbf{f}'_b  is feasible. Hence its value  |\mathbf{f}'_b| = |\mathbf{f}^*_a|  cannot exceed the maximum flow in  \mathcal{N}_b , proving  \text{MaxFlow}(\mathbf{C}_a, G_a) \le \text{MaxFlow}(\mathbf{C}_b, G_b) . Normalizing by  \Phi_{\text{target}}  yields the result. ∎

By the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem, the autonomy function can be equivalently expressed via minimum cuts, providing additional intuition.

6.3 Safety Guarantee

Theorem 3: Under the feasibility check (Section 7.5), no action is taken that violates any hard constraint.

Proof: The feasibility check explicitly verifies constraints; if any fails, NO_OP is selected, which is trivially safe. ∎

6.4 Finite-Sample Regret Bound for CPW-MCTS

Theorem 4 (Regret Bound): Under CPW-MCTS with progressive widening parameters  c_{\text{pw}}, \alpha_{\text{pw}}  and PUCT constant  c_{\text{puct}} , the expected cumulative regret after  N  simulations satisfies:

\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{R}_N] = \mathcal{O}\left( c_{\text{pw}} \cdot |V_H|^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \cdot \ln(N) + \sum_{k=1}^K \lambda_k \, \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}_{k,N}] \right)

where  \mathcal{C}_{k,N}  is the cumulative constraint violation for constraint  k .

Proof sketch: Progressive widening limits the effective branching factor to  B(N) = \lfloor c_{\text{pw}} N^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \rfloor . Standard UCT regret bounds apply within each expanded action set, and the widening schedule ensures all actions are eventually explored. The Lagrange multiplier terms account for penalized objective selection. A full proof is given in Appendix B.

Remark: The bound improves on naive UCT applied to the full dynamic action space, which would suffer exponential blowup.

