Chapter 7: Algorithmic Framework

7.1 Dual-Mode Bottleneck Identification

The bottleneck at time  t  is identified via a hybrid approach.

7.1.1 Continuous Sensitivity

The continuous bottleneck evaluates marginal throughput gains from incremental capacity increases:

\frac{\partial \Phi(G_t)}{\partial C_{i,t}} \approx \frac{\Phi(G_t \mid C_{i,t} + \Delta C) - \Phi(G_t \mid C_{i,t})}{\Delta C}

Since  \Phi  is computed via max-flow, this can be efficiently calculated using dual variables (shadow prices) from the linear programming formulation:

\frac{\partial \Phi(G_t)}{\partial C_{i,t}} = \frac{1}{\Phi_{\text{target}}} \cdot \mu_i

where  \mu_i  is the dual variable associated with node  i 's capacity constraint.

7.1.2 Discrete Graph-Expansion Sensitivity

For discrete node additions, compute:

\Delta \Phi_{\text{discrete}}(v) = \Phi(G_t \cup \{v\}) - \Phi(G_t)

7.1.3 Hybrid Action Scoring

The macro-level planner ranks candidate capabilities using:

\text{Score}(v) = \omega_1 \cdot \Delta \Phi_{\text{discrete}}(v) + \omega_2 \cdot \max_{i \in \text{parents}(v)} \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial C_{i,t}} + \omega_3 \cdot \text{Resilience}(v)

with weights  \omega_j \ge 0, \sum \omega_j = 1 . Defaults:  \omega_1=0.5, \omega_2=0.3, \omega_3=0.2 . This score serves as the prior  P(s,a)  for CPW-MCTS.

7.2 Hierarchical Planning Architecture

The planner operates on two levels:

· Level 1 (Strategic): Macro-step  \Delta T = 30  lunar days. Selects next capability  v_{\text{target}}  via CPW-MCTS.
· Level 2 (Operational): Micro-step  \Delta t = 1  hour. Determines resource allocation  \mathbf{r}_{\text{alloc}}  via Model Predictive Control with linear programming.

7.3 Constrained Progressive Widening MCTS (CPW-MCTS)

CPW-MCTS addresses the non-stationary action space. At state  s_t , valid actions are:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(s) \triangleq \{ v \in V_H \setminus V(G_t) \mid \text{parents}(v) \subseteq V(G_t) \} \cup \{\text{NO\_OP}\}

Progressive widening restricts the expanded action set:

|\mathcal{A}_{\text{tree}}(s)| \le \lfloor c_{\text{pw}} \cdot N(s)^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \rfloor

with  c_{\text{pw}} > 0 ,  \alpha_{\text{pw}} \in (0,1) .

7.3.1 Dynamic Prior Heuristic

When expanding a new action, candidate ranking uses the hybrid sensitivity prior:

P(s,a) = \frac{\exp(\text{Score}(a)/\tau)}{\sum_{b \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}} \setminus \mathcal{A}_{\text{tree}}} \exp(\text{Score}(b)/\tau)}

with temperature  \tau > 0 .

7.3.2 Constrained Action Selection Objective

Within visited tree nodes, action selection balances expected return  \bar{Q}_R , constraint penalties  \bar{Q}_{C_k} , and exploration:

a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{tree}}(s)} \left[ \bar{Q}_R(s,a) - \sum_{k=1}^K \lambda_k \bar{Q}_{C_k}(s,a) + c_{\text{puct}} \cdot P(s,a) \frac{\sqrt{N(s)}}{1+N(s,a)} \right]

7.3.3 Algorithm Pseudocode

```
Algorithm: CPW-MCTS(s_t, H, budget, c_pw, α_pw, c_puct, λ, ω)
1:  Initialize root with state s_t, visits=1, tree_actions={NO_OP}
2:  Compute priors P for all valid actions
3:  for sim = 1 to budget:
4:      node = root; path = [node]; actions_taken = []
5:      while not terminal:
6:          valid = A_valid(node.state)
7:          max_allowed = floor(c_pw * node.visits^α_pw)
8:          if |node.tree_actions| < max_allowed and |node.tree_actions| < |valid|:
9:              Sample new action a from unexpanded using priors
10:             Add to tree_actions, initialize Q, N
11:             next_state = transition(node.state, a)
12:             child = new Node(next_state)
13:             node.children[a] = child; node = child; break
14:         else:
15:             Select a* using constrained PUCT formula
16:             node = node.children[a*]
17:     R_value, C_values = rollout(node.state)
18:     Backpropagate along path
19: return argmax_a root.N_a[a]
```

7.3.4 Default Policy (Rollout)

The rollout policy is a hybrid TOC-greedy heuristic with  \epsilon_{\text{rollout}} = 0.1  random exploration.

7.3.5 Complexity and Scaling

Space Complexity:  \mathcal{O}(\text{budget})  nodes, each storing  \mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{A}_{\text{tree}}|)  statistics; with progressive widening, sub-exponential growth.

Time Complexity per simulation: selection  \mathcal{O}(\text{depth} \times |\mathcal{A}_{\text{tree}}|) , expansion  \mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}| \log |\mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}|) , rollout  \mathcal{O}(\text{rollout\_depth} \times \text{maxflow}) , backprop  \mathcal{O}(\text{depth}) . The dominant cost is max-flow during rollouts, polynomial in  |V_t| .

Runtime Estimate: For a 12-node graph, one max-flow computation is negligible (~µs). For 1000 simulations per macro-step, with rollout depth 50, each simulation may perform up to 50 max-flow calls, giving ~50k max-flow calls per decision. At 10 µs each, that's 0.5 s per decision, or ~1 minute per full run (122 macro-steps). For 900 runs, ~15 hours on a single core. This is feasible; parallelization reduces wall-clock time further.

7.4 Lagrangian Policy Iteration

Lagrange multipliers are updated after each macro-step using the estimated constraint returns:

\lambda_k \leftarrow \max(0, \lambda_k + \eta_\lambda (J_{C_k} - c_k))

7.5 Feasibility Projection

A hard feasibility check is applied to any candidate action before execution:

```
Algorithm: Feasibility-Check(s_t, a_t)
1:  Check mass: ||m_import||_1 ≤ M_max - E_t
2:  Check energy: E_required ≤ E_available
3:  Check prerequisites: parents(v_target) ⊆ V_t
4:  Check health: min(K_i) ≥ K_min for i in prereq(v_target)
5:  Check allocation: sum(r_alloc) ≤ 1.0
6:  Check instantaneous risk: Risk(a_t) ≤ Risk_max
7:  return all checks passed
```

If any check fails, the fallback NO_OP action is selected. This hard check guarantees local constraint satisfaction, while the Lagrangian terms in the objective promote global feasibility of the expected trajectory.

