A.2 Proof of Theorem 2 (Monotonicity of Autonomy Function)

Theorem 2 (Monotonicity under Graph Inclusion):
Let  H = (V_H, E_H)  be the total capability dependency hypergraph. Let  G_a = (V_a, E_a)  and  G_b = (V_b, E_b)  be two active capability subgraphs with operational capacity vectors  \mathbf{C}_a = \{C_{v,a}\}_{v \in V_a}  and  \mathbf{C}_b = \{C_{v,b}\}_{v \in V_b} . If  G_a \subseteq G_b  (i.e.,  V_a \subseteq V_b  and  E_a \subseteq E_b ) and capacities are pointwise non-decreasing ( \forall v \in V_a, \; C_{v,a} \le C_{v,b} ), then

\Phi(G_a) \le \Phi(G_b).

Proof:
Recall that the autonomy function is defined as the normalized maximum flow through the augmented flow network  \mathcal{N}(G, \mathbf{C}) , which is constructed by splitting each node  v  into  v_{\text{in}}  and  v_{\text{out}}  with an internal capacity edge  (v_{\text{in}}, v_{\text{out}})  of capacity  C_{v} . A virtual source  s  connects to all source nodes  V_{\text{src}}  with capacity  C_{u} , and all sink nodes  V_{\text{snk}}  connect to virtual sink  t^*  with capacity  C_{v}^{\text{required}} . Internal edges  (u_{\text{out}}, v_{\text{in}})  have capacity  \min(C_u, C_v) .

Let  \mathcal{N}_a = \mathcal{N}(G_a, \mathbf{C}_a)  and  \mathcal{N}_b = \mathcal{N}(G_b, \mathbf{C}_b) . We need to show  \text{MaxFlow}(\mathcal{N}_a) \le \text{MaxFlow}(\mathcal{N}_b) .

Step 1: Optimal flow in  \mathcal{N}_a .
Let  \mathbf{f}^*_a  be an optimal flow vector in  \mathcal{N}_a , so

|\mathbf{f}^*_a| = \text{MaxFlow}(\mathcal{N}_a) = \sum_{v \in V_{\text{snk}} \cap V_a} f^*_a(v_{\text{out}}, t^*).

Step 2: Construct a candidate flow  \mathbf{f}'_b  in  \mathcal{N}_b .
Define  \mathbf{f}'_b  by extending  \mathbf{f}^*_a  with zero flow on all edges that are in  \mathcal{N}_b  but not in  \mathcal{N}_a :

f'_b(u,v) = \begin{cases}
f^*_a(u,v) & \text{if } (u,v) \in E_a' \\
0 & \text{if } (u,v) \in E_b' \setminus E_a'
\end{cases}

where  E_a'  and  E_b'  denote the augmented edge sets (including source/sink connections and internal split edges).

Step 3: Feasibility of  \mathbf{f}'_b  in  \mathcal{N}_b .

· Capacity constraints:
    For any edge  (u,v) \in E_a' , we have
  f'_b(u,v) = f^*_a(u,v) \le c_a(u,v) \le c_b(u,v),
  because capacities in  \mathcal{N}_b  are pointwise no smaller than in  \mathcal{N}_a  (by assumption  \mathbf{C}_a \le \mathbf{C}_b  and  E_a \subseteq E_b ). For new edges  (u,v) \in E_b' \setminus E_a' ,  f'_b(u,v) = 0 \le c_b(u,v) .
· Conservation constraints:
    For any node  v \in V_a  (excluding  s  and  t^* ), the flow conservation holds in  \mathcal{N}_a :
  \sum_{u: (u,v_{\text{in}}) \in E_a'} f^*_a(u,v_{\text{in}}) = f^*_a(v_{\text{in}}, v_{\text{out}}) = \sum_{w: (v_{\text{out}},w) \in E_a'} f^*_a(v_{\text{out}}, w).
  Since  \mathbf{f}'_b  equals  \mathbf{f}^*_a  on all these edges and adds zero on new edges, the same conservation holds in  \mathcal{N}_b  for all original nodes. For newly added nodes  v \in V_b \setminus V_a , all incident edges carry zero flow, so conservation trivially holds.

Step 4: Value comparison.
The value of  \mathbf{f}'_b  is

|\mathbf{f}'_b| = \sum_{v \in V_{\text{snk}} \cap V_b} f'_b(v_{\text{out}}, t^*) = \sum_{v \in V_{\text{snk}} \cap V_a} f^*_a(v_{\text{out}}, t^*) = |\mathbf{f}^*_a|,

because zero flow is added for sinks in  V_b \setminus V_a .

Since  \mathbf{f}'_b  is a feasible flow in  \mathcal{N}_b , the maximum flow in  \mathcal{N}_b  is at least  |\mathbf{f}'_b| :

\text{MaxFlow}(\mathcal{N}_b) \ge |\mathbf{f}'_b| = \text{MaxFlow}(\mathcal{N}_a).

Normalizing by  \Phi_{\text{target}} > 0  yields  \Phi(G_a) \le \Phi(G_b) . \blacksquare

Min-Cut Interpretation:
By the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem, the result can also be seen by noting that any cut in  \mathcal{N}_b  has capacity at least as large as the corresponding cut in  \mathcal{N}_a , and new cuts have non‑negative capacity. Hence the minimum cut capacity is non-decreasing.

