A.3 Proof of Theorem 3 (Safety Guarantee)

Theorem 3: Under the constraint enforcement in the feasibility check, no action is taken that violates any hard constraint.

Proof:
The feasibility check (Algorithm in Section 7.5) is applied to every candidate action  a_t  before execution. It verifies:

· Mass constraint:  \|\mathbf{m}_{\text{import}}\|_1 \le M_{\max} - E_t .
· Energy constraint:  E_{\text{required}}(a_t) \le E_{\text{available}}(s_t) .
· Prerequisite constraint:  \text{parents}(v_{\text{target}}) \subseteq V_t .
· Health constraint:  \min_{i \in \text{prereq}(v_{\text{target}})} K_{i,t} \ge K_{\min} .
· Allocation constraint:  \sum_i r_{\text{alloc},i} \le 1 .
· Risk constraint:  \text{Risk}(a_t) \le \text{Risk}_{\max} .

If any of these conditions fail, the algorithm returns the fallback action NO_OP, which trivially satisfies all constraints (as shown in Theorem 1). Therefore, the executed action always satisfies the hard constraints. \blacksquare

Remark: This guarantee is instantaneous (per step) and does not by itself ensure global (trajectory-level) constraint satisfaction in expectation. The Lagrangian penalty in the CMDP objective is designed to promote global feasibility, but finite-sample convergence is approximate (see Limitation 6 in Chapter 14).

