A.4 Proof of Theorem 4 (Finite-Sample Regret Bound for CPW-MCTS)

Theorem 4 (Regret Bound): Under CPW-MCTS with progressive widening parameters  c_{\text{pw}} > 0, \alpha_{\text{pw}} \in (0,1) , and PUCT constant  c_{\text{puct}} > 0 , the expected cumulative regret after  N  simulations over a meta-graph  H  with  |V_H|  nodes satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{R}_N] = \mathcal{O}\left( c_{\text{pw}} \, |V_H|^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \ln N + \sum_{k=1}^K \lambda_k \, \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}_{k,N}] \right),

where  \mathcal{C}_{k,N}  is the cumulative constraint violation for constraint  k  over  N  simulations.

Proof Sketch:
We outline the main steps; a fully rigorous proof requires additional technical assumptions (e.g., bounded rewards, i.i.d. rollout samples) that are standard in MCTS analysis.

1. Effective Branching Factor Control.
      At any tree node with visit count  n , the number of expanded actions is bounded by  B(n) = \lfloor c_{\text{pw}} n^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \rfloor . This contrasts with the full candidate action set size  |\mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(s)| \le |V_H| . Thus, the tree branching factor grows sublinearly with visits, preventing exponential blowup.
2. Regret Decomposition.
      The cumulative regret relative to the optimal (constrained) policy can be decomposed into:
   · Regret due to not expanding all actions immediately (exploration vs. exploitation).
   · Regret due to using a penalized objective  \bar{Q}_R - \sum_k \lambda_k \bar{Q}_{C_k} .
3. Standard UCT Regret Bound within Expanded Action Set.
      For a fixed set of  B  actions, the PUCT algorithm achieves regret  \mathcal{O}(B \ln n)  (Kocsis & Szepesvári, 2006; Auer et al., 2002). Integrating over the growing action set yields  \mathcal{O}(c_{\text{pw}} n^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \ln n) . Summing over all nodes visited during the  N  simulations gives the first term.
4. Constraint Penalty Regret.
      The Lagrangian term  \sum_k \lambda_k \bar{Q}_{C_k}  biases action selection away from constraint-violating actions. The additional regret incurred is proportional to the product of  \lambda_k  and the cumulative constraint violation  \mathcal{C}_{k,N} . Under the assumption that the Lagrange multipliers are updated conservatively (bounded), this term is  \mathcal{O}(\sum_k \lambda_k \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}_{k,N}]) .
5. Total Bound.
      Summing over  N  simulations and all nodes, and using the fact that  |V_H|  bounds the maximum number of candidate actions at any node, we obtain
   \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{R}_N] \le C_1 \, c_{\text{pw}} |V_H|^{\alpha_{\text{pw}}} \ln N + C_2 \sum_k \lambda_k \, \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}_{k,N}],
   for constants  C_1, C_2  independent of  N  and  |V_H| . \blacksquare

Assumptions for Rigorous Proof:

· Rewards and constraint costs are bounded in absolute value.
· Rollout policy yields i.i.d. estimates of state values.
· Progressive widening schedule satisfies  \sum_{n=1}^\infty (B(n)/n)^2 < \infty  (which holds for  \alpha_{\text{pw}} < 1/2 , but our default  \alpha_{\text{pw}}=0.5  is borderline; in practice we may choose  \alpha_{\text{pw}} < 0.5  for theoretical guarantees).
· The Lagrange multipliers remain bounded (which can be enforced by clipping).

A complete proof with explicit constants is beyond the scope of this dissertation and is left as future theoretical work.

