C.2 Sensitivity Analysis Ranges

All mass and power values in the meta-graph are treated as nominal with ±30% uncertainty bounds. The sensitivity analysis perturbs each parameter independently within these bounds using a uniform distribution. Additionally, the prerequisite edges are perturbed by randomly adding or removing edges with probability 0.1 (subject to maintaining DAG property). This tests robustness of the planner to structural uncertainty.

C.3 Baseline Policy Formulations

C.3.1 Baseline E: Globally Optimized Static Sequence (MILP)

The MILP baseline is formulated as a shortest-path problem over the state graph of all possible capability deployment sequences under nominal (deterministic) dynamics. The state is the set of deployed capabilities  V_t \subseteq V_H . Actions add one capability at a time (satisfying prerequisites). Transition costs are the mass import of the added capability. The objective is to maximize the final autonomy  \Phi(V_T)  while minimizing total mass. A weighted sum objective is used:

\min \left( \sum_{t} \|\mathbf{m}_{\text{import},t}\|_1 - \lambda \Phi(V_T) \right)

where  \lambda  is chosen to balance mass and autonomy. Because the graph has 12 nodes, exhaustive enumeration of all feasible sequences (subject to prerequisites) is feasible; we compute the optimal open-loop sequence offline.

C.3.2 Baseline F: Model Predictive Control (MPC)

The MPC baseline re-plans at each macro-step by solving a one-step lookahead optimization:

a_t^{\text{MPC}} = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(s_t)} \left[ \Phi(s_{t+1}) - \alpha \|\mathbf{m}_{\text{import}}\|_1 \right]

where  s_{t+1}  is the deterministic predicted next state (using nominal transition model without stochastic faults). This provides a closed-loop, myopic baseline that does not use tree search.

