D.2 Fault Injection Scenario Details

SC-01 Nominal Baseline: No disturbances; only natural degradation.

SC-02 Abrasive Wear Shock: At  t = 120  days, excavation health begins decreasing at an additional rate of  0.02 \, \text{day}^{-1}  for 60 days, then recovers. This is modeled by increasing  \alpha_{\text{excav}}  temporarily.

SC-03 Lunar Night Disruption: Every 29.5 days, power generation drops to 20% for 14 days (simulating lunar night). This reduces available energy for all operations.

SC-04 Supply Chain Blackout: At  t = 180  days, a random event reduces the Earth import capacity by 75% for 90 days (simulating a launch failure). The planner must adapt to reduced mass availability.

SC-05 Multi-Subsystem Failure: At  t = 300  days, excavation and refining health simultaneously drop to 0.2 (simulating a catastrophic event). The planner must prioritize recovery.

D.3 Statistical Test Formulas

Paired t-test for comparing AGM between proposed planner and each baseline across replicates:

t = \frac{\bar{d}}{s_d / \sqrt{n}},

where  \bar{d}  is the mean difference,  s_d  the standard deviation of differences,  n=10  replicates.

Cohen's d effect size:

d = \frac{\bar{X}_1 - \bar{X}_2}{s_{\text{pooled}}}

with pooled standard deviation.

Bootstrap confidence intervals: 1000 resamples with replacement, computing the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles.

D.4 Summary of Expected Results (Placeholder)

The actual results will be populated after running the experiments. Based on preliminary theoretical analysis, we expect:

· Under SC-01 (nominal), all planners should achieve similar final autonomy, but the proposed planner may find sequences with lower mass import due to better long-term planning.
· Under SC-02 through SC-05, the proposed planner should outperform static baselines (A, E) and myopic baselines (B, C, D) in terms of recovery time and AGM, because it can anticipate future bottlenecks and adapt to failures.
· The MPC baseline (F) may perform comparably in some scenarios but should lag under stochastic disturbances because it does not account for uncertainty in its one-step lookahead.
· Constraint violation rates for the proposed planner should be zero for hard constraints (due to feasibility check) and low for soft constraints (due to Lagrangian penalties).

